  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1255 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCan't rule out a few showers and perhaps a stray thunderstorm\nacross western NM on Friday as southwest flow, and arguably a very\nweak shortwave, crosses the northwest half of the state. A\ndeepening lee side trough should increase south to southwest winds\nover northeast NM with gusts around 30-35 mph. Overall though, \nshould be a pretty quiet day with temperatures within a degree or\ntwo of Thursday's readings.\n\nModels continue to indicate that Hurricane Rachel will be sheared\napart and absorbed into a Pacific low west of the Baja Peninsula\nSaturday. That low, and the moisture remnants of Rachel, look to\nswing into SoCal and SW AZ Sunday while an upper low over the \nPacNW digs southward over the west coast. Thus, Rachel's moisture\nremnants look to stay west of NM. Nonetheless, southwest flow \nover NM will gradually advect some moisture into western and \nportions of central NM over the weekend. PWATs will trend upward \nto around 0.7 to 0.9 inches. Scattered to numerous showers and \nisolated thunderstorms will develop Saturday afternoon and persist\nthrough Sunday night. Though some areas could certainly pick up \nan inch or so of rainfall through this period, these heavier \namounts should be spotty. \n\nTropical Depression Twenty-E has developed south of Mexico today.\nThis system is forecast to slowly shift northwest and north over\nthe next few days, then the big question remains on where it will\ngo early next week. There is still large discrepancies between\noperational models, AI models and ensemble outputs. Both the\noperational GFS and EC continue to bring the tropical system\nnorthward with moisture from the system shifting into NM Monday\nnight with rain continuing through at least Tuesday. Ensemble\nmodels continue to show little, if any, increase in PWATs during\nthe period, which suggests the remnants will be diverted to the\nsoutheast. The AI models are closer to this solution as well as\nthey keep much drier conditions over NM. It should also be noted\nthat the aforementioned Pacific low digging down the coast will\nalso begin to shift east-northeastward during the Monday night\nthru Wed timeframe. This will not only have an effect on where the\ntropical remnants go, but the low itself should bring additional\nshower and thunderstorm chances to NM as it crosses the Four\nCorners Wednesday.",
